# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HealThaali — line endings and diff behaviour
#
# Windows defaults `core.autocrlf` to true, which rewrites every file it touches
# to CRLF on checkout. That is what produced the "LF will be replaced by CRLF"
# warnings on the initial commit: a fresh clone would come back with a working
# tree full of phantom modifications, and a `git diff` that shows every line of
# every file as changed even when nothing was.
#
# These rules pin the behaviour to what the repository already stores, so what
# you check out is what CI builds.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF *inside the repository*. `eol=lf` is the part
# that keeps the working copy LF as well, on Windows included.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

* text=auto eol=lf

# The usual advice is to give *.bat an `eol=crlf` exception. This repository
# deliberately does not, because the exception was measured and gives nothing:
# cmd.exe runs LF batch files fine -- run.bat, build.bat and preview.bat have all
# been executed with LF line endings and worked -- and `git checkout` does not
# rewrite a file whose stat cache says it is unchanged, so the rule stayed
# latent while the working copy remained LF anyway. A rule that describes
# something other than reality is worse than no rule. One ending everywhere.

# Binary assets: never line-ending converted, never diffed as text. `text=auto`
# usually detects these on its own, but small PNGs and icons occasionally fall
# through the heuristic, and a corrupted image is silent until it reaches the
# browser.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
